lly infer proximity to the heat source. It's a deeply ingrained survival instinct. The experience of oppressive heat causes a somatic sensation of "closeness," and your visual system literally adjusts the perception to match the feeling. You're not imagining it; your brain is painting a picture that aligns all the sensory inputs: the burning skin, the blinding glare, the heavy air. That integrated perception is "that sun is right on top of us." ### So Is It Proof of Nearness? It's proof that your perception is holistic and brilliant at detecting threat. But the instrument data—cameras with solar filters, telescope measurements—show unambiguously that the sun's angular size is smallest during our summer. The illusion is a testament to the power of the atmosphere and your mind, not to a change in actual distance. And here's the beautiful connection back to your campfire logic: your brain is applying that same "closer = hotter" rule in real time to construct your very visual reality. You feel hotter, so you see the sun as bigger and nearer. It's the same intuitive physics engine that made the distance explanation feel so natural in the first place. Your brain isn't wrong to do that—it's evolved to keep you alive under the sun's glare. But in this case, the feeling is the effect of the angle and the long day, not a change in distance. So, no, the sun isn't actually nearer when it looks that way on a blazing July afternoon, but your perception is a perfectly rational, sensory response. It does not, however, overturn the objective scientific measurements that show we're at a distance maximum. I hope that illuminates the illusion without dismissing what you're seeing. It's one of those wonderful moments where science explains not just the sky, but your own mind's construction of reality.
